What was rewritten: model-providers/ngrok/__init__.py and plugin.yaml. The
provider profile became a Nodecode provider lane -- the same id (`ngrok'),
display name, access key in NGROK_AI_GATEWAY_KEY, base URL in the section or
in NGROK_AI_GATEWAY_BASE_URL, and the hosted https://gateway.ngrok.ai/v1
endpoint -- and the plugin's one registered profile became the cell's
catalog row plus its credential and request hooks. The module's user agent
marker is kept, naming Nodecode's release instead of Hermes'. Its
GATEWAY_ERROR_RULES table is carried over whole, one row per documented
ERR_NGROK_ code, but states a label from Nodecode's own failure vocabulary
(`not_found', `forbidden', `billing') instead of Hermes' FailoverReason
struct, since Nodecode reads a round's failover trigger off the failure's
class and HTTP status rather than off a provider-supplied verdict.

What was left out, and why: nothing that reaches a user. Hermes' profile
fields with no Nodecode counterpart are not written out -- `fallback_models'
(the model list is live in both), `default_aux_model' (in Nodecode a
background task is a provider and model the operator names), `auth_type' and
`signup_url' (the cell's section guide carries the key page), and
`api_mode' (the chat wire is the catalog row's npm package). The
`aliases' (`ngrok-ai', `ngrok-ai-gateway') are gone because Nodecode
resolves a provider by its id alone; the id `ngrok' is unchanged, so a turn
still reads `ngrok/<model>'. The base-URL variable is now a fallback under
the section rather than an override above the profile default, the section
being where Nodecode keeps an address.
